WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.460
You may have heard
the expression

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.500
“knowledge is power.”

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.320
Well, today we’re going
to give you more power

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.180
to control your
diet and lifestyle

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.460
by giving you the facts.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.560
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.620
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.220
Today we have the first
in an eight-part series

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.400
on how industries impact
dietary and health guidelines.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.500
And we start with Big Sugar.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.240
In 2019, a series of reviews
was published in the

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.960
Annals of Internal Medicine
that concluded the same thing

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.740
that past reviews have concluded:
adherence to dietary patterns

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:41.940
lower in red or processed meat
intake may result in decreased risk

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.660
for premature death, cardiometabolic
disease and mortality,

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.780
meaning the risk of getting
and dying of diseases

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.080
like heart disease and type 2
diabetes, as well as the risk

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.600
of getting cancer
and dying from cancer,

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.740
therefore, they concluded in their
Dietary Guideline Recommendations,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:02.160
“continue [your] current
red meat consumption,”

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.580
“continue [your] processed
meat consumption.”

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:11.460
Wait, what?! Yeah, premature
death, cancer, heart disease,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.340
diabetes, but keep eating
your burgers and bacon.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.460
To understand what just happened,
we have to go back to 2015.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.760
The Dietary Guidelines
had just had the audacity

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.460
to recommend people
reduce their sugar intake.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.940
Imagine you work
for the sugar industry.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.260
The evidence is overwhelmingly
against you; what do you do?

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.440
Well, what did the
tobacco industry do?

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.100
One method involved the
tobacco industry’s funding of

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.760
and involvement in seemingly
unbiased scientific groups

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:48.720
to manipulate the scientific
debate concerning tobacco and health.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.520
Groups like the ILSI, the
International Life Sciences Institute,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.660
which has enjoyed a long
and serious collaboration

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.220
with the tobacco industry,

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:04.800
that same industry group, shapes
food policy around the world.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.160
The International Life
Sciences Institute (ILSI),

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.340
technically a nonprofit with
an innocuous sounding name

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.040
“has been quietly
infiltrating government health

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.960
and nutrition bodies
around the world.”

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.680
It was created by a top
Coca‑Cola executive, and

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.680
“almost entirely funded by
Goliaths of the agribusiness,

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.780
food, and pharmaceutical industries.”

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.140
“After decades largely
operating under the radar,

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.260
the Institute is coming
under increasing scrutiny

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.080
by health advocates in the
United States and abroad who say

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.260
it's little more than a front
group advancing the interests

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.980
of the 400 corporate members,”
among them Coke and Pepsi.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.020
So, when the 2015 dietary
guidelines advised eating less sugar,

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.560
the soda-funded International
Life Sciences Institute

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.740
sponsored a review
concluding that the guidelines

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.320
on sugar were simply not trustworthy.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:00.720
Who did they pick for this
hatchet job? Bradley Johnston.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.180
And here it is, published in
the Annals of Internal Medicine.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.840
The Scientiﬁc Basis of Guideline
Recommendations on Sugar Intake,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.080
and concluded there basically wasn’t one.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.700
“Guidelines on dietary sugar do
not meet criteria for trustworthy

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.940
recommendations as they are
based on low-quality evidence.”

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.500
“This comes right out of
the tobacco industry’s playbook:

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.160
cast doubt on the science,”
said Professor Marion Nestle,

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.940
“This is a classic (shameful) example of
how industry funding biases opinion."

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:36.660
Yes, the paper was
paid for by the likes of

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.260
Coca-Cola, Hershey, Red Bull,
and the makers of Oreos.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.020
But the authors swore
they wrote the protocol

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.540
and conducted the study independently.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.820
It turns out that was a lie,
forcing the journal to publish

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.880
a corrected version after the
Associated Press obtained emails

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:57.240
showing the industry front
group “requested revisions.”

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.680
It also came out that a co-author
conveniently forgot to mention

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.060
a $25 grand grant she got
directly from Coca-Cola.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:11.460
You know it’s bad when
candy bar companies criticize

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.680
an industry-funded paper on sugar.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.600
The maker of Snickers, Skittles,
and M&amp;M’s broke ranks with

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:21.840
other food companies and
denounced the industry-funded paper.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.520
They themselves were a member
of the ILSI, but come on,

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.160
telling people to ignore
guidelines to cut down on sugar?

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.860
That’s just making us all look bad.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:35.040
If you look at the relationship
between funding source

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.100
and the conclusions in nutrition-
related scientific articles,

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.100
there’s like seven or eight times
the odds that the conclusion

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.780
will skew favorably compared to
studies with no industry funding.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.860
For interventional studies, the
proportion of studies paid for

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:53.460
by industry that reached unfavorable
conclusions about their own

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.500
products was a big fat 0 percent,
which should not surprise anyone.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.760
So, what can journals do
to counteract the tactics

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.520
that industry often uses to
advocate for the safety

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.760
of unsafe products, or
question the integrity of science

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.440
that calls their products into question?

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.360
To combat the tobacco industry's
inﬂuence over scientiﬁc discourse,

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.980
leading journal editors have
refused to be passive conduits

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:21.900
for articles funded by
the tobacco industry.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:26.040
They just won’t accept tobacco
industry-funded studies period.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.700
Accordingly, high-quality
journals could refrain

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:32.580
from publishing studies on
health effects of added sugars

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.420
funded by soda and cookie
companies. But they’re not.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:40.020
This was published in the
Annals of Internal Medicine.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:45.000
And so, four years later, with the
next batch of dietary guidelines

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.460
on the way, and the
last scientific report

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:50.100
of the guidelines committee
encouraging people to eat diets

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.280
not just lower in sugar
but lower in meat as well.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:58.140
Big Beef decided to follow in
the footsteps of Big Butterfinger.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:02.160
Same journal, same guy,
same scientist-for-hire,

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.360
Bradley Johnston, as lead
author, and the rest is history.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:12.611
We’ll dig into exactly
how he pulled it off next.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.760
In 2016, a Washington-based lobby
group published a scientific review

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.000
which concluded that evidence
in favor of guidelines

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.260
recommending limits on added
dietary sugar was “low quality”

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:28.920
and did not “meet criteria
for trustworthy recommendations.”

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.140
This was a group funded by multinational
food and agrochemical companies

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:38.520
from Coca-Cola to Monsanto to
the captains of corn syrup,

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:43.140
accused of “hijacking the scientific
process in a disingenuous way

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.900
to sow doubt and
jeopardize public health.”

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:50.640
But exactly how did
they get away with it?

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.000
Here’s their paper,
questioning the scientiﬁc basis

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.340
of guideline recommendations
on sugar intake.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:01.200
Using the “GRADE” approach, they
concluded that the overall quality

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.560
of evidence to support such
recommendations was low to very low.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.480
GRADE stands for the Grading
of Recommendations Assessment,

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:14.100
Development and Evaluation initiative,
which was developed to make

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:19.080
clinical guidelines more evidence-
based and thank heavens for that.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.500
Clinical guidelines, like
which drug to give to whom,

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:26.040
used to be developed by
something like the GOBSAT method,

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:29.400
(just some Good Old Boys
Sitting Around a Table).

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:33.120
Under GRADE, high-quality
evidence appears

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:36.540
to be derived exclusively
from randomized trials.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:40.020
This is not surprising given that
the GRADE process was designed

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.160
for drugs, and, of course, you
want your drugs put to the test.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:47.760
Like remember the Premarin
story, where for a decade,

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.069
organizations recommended
that clinicians encourage

00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:54.060
postmenopausal women to use
hormone replacement therapy,

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.480
because women who were taking it
appeared to have fewer heart attacks.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:00.120
But when put to the test,

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:03.780
randomized controlled trials
instead showed the opposite.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:08.040
So, we definitely want drugs put
through randomized double-blind,

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:10.560
placebo-controlled trials,
where people are randomized

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.980
to take the drug or unknowingly
take a placebo instead,

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:15.660
and no one knows
who’s in which group

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:18.900
until the code is broken at
the end, and you see how well

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:21.300
the two groups did
compared to each other.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:24.960
Everyone agrees that
randomized controlled trials

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.320
are enormously valuable in
many areas of medical research,

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:31.260
such as the testing of drugs,
but wait a second,

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.600
how do you do that with diet?

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.400
There’s no such thing
as a placebo diet.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.920
How can you randomize
people to different diets

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:40.860
but somehow hide from them
which diet they’re following?

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:44.760
People tend to notice what
they put in their own mouths.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:48.300
And RCTs (randomized controlled
trials) are so expensive

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:50.820
they typically last for
only weeks or months,

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.640
but it takes most cancers
decades to develop;

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.880
so, it’s nearly impossible
to see if different diets

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.500
prevent or cause
cancer that way.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:03.360
To see if smoking causes cancer,
you can follow large cohorts

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:07.020
of smokers and nonsmokers out
for decades to see who gets cancer

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:09.900
and who doesn’t, but it’s not
like you can randomize people

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.000
to smoke or not smoke for decades.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.140
When studying lifestyle interventions,
you often just can’t do

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.900
randomized controlled trials,
and even if you could,

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.520
it might not be ethical.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:24.600
Take crib death, for example,
sudden infant death syndrome.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:28.320
A pivotal study studying the
habits of infants who died

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.100
discovered that sleeping on
their stomachs was a risk factor;

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:36.180
so, we started educating
parents “face up to wake up,”

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:40.920
and the SIDS rates dropped.
What more do we need?

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:45.000
Would it even be ethical to randomize
thousands of babies to sleep

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.760
on their backs or fronts and
count up the deaths at this point?

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:52.860
You can get sufficient evidence
to make lifestyle recommendations

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:56.520
that save lives without
randomized controlled trials.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:00.960
Critical questions, like
“What dietary pattern produces

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.120
the best health outcomes
over a lifetime?”

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:06.960
simply can’t be answered with
randomized controlled trials.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.960
You can’t randomize people
to different lifetime diets

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.520
and expect them to stick to them;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:16.440
so, we need to compile an amalgamation
of evidence from other study designs.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:19.920
The lack of evidence from
randomized controlled trials

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:22.800
has become a common
argumentation strategy

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.960
for criticizing nutrition
recommendations.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:30.480
Similar claims were made by
the tobacco industry in its attempt

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:33.300
to discredit evidence
on the harms of tobacco.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:38.160
Case in point: the so-called
“sound science” campaign

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.380
carried out by Philip Morris.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.620
This is all straight out of
the tobacco industry playbook.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:47.220
Philip Morris used public relations
firms and lawyers to develop

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:51.360
a “sound science” program that
involved recruiting other industries

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:54.600
and issues to obscure the
tobacco industry’s role.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:59.700
They tried to enshrine
“good epidemiological practices”

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:03.240
that would make it impossible to
conclude that secondhand smoke—

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:06.960
and thus other environmental
toxins—caused diseases.

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:10.620
“Public health professionals
need to be aware that

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:14.040
the ’sound science’ movement
is not an indigenous effort

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:16.380
from within the profession
to improve the quality

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:20.280
of scientific discourse, but
rather reflects sophisticated

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:23.640
public relations campaigns
controlled by industry executives

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:27.660
and lawyers whose aim is to
manipulate the standards of

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:31.380
scientific proof to serve the
corporate interests of their clients,”

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:34.620
and not just tobacco clients
but they hope to expand

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:37.080
to the food, plastics,
and chemical industries.

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:41.760
Philip Morris went beyond just
“creating doubt” and “controversy”

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:45.000
about the scientific evidence,
to attempting to change

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:47.160
the scientific standards of proof.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:49.740
But randomized
controlled trials aren’t

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:51.360
the only source
of good evidence.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:56.940
As Sir Bradford Hill himself
said, who pioneered the randomized

00:11:56.940 --> 00:12:00.720
controlled trial: “Any belief
that the controlled trial

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.340
is the only way to
go would mean

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:05.400
not just that the pendulum
had swung too far

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:10.321
but that it had come
right off (the) hook.”

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:14.220
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:16.980
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:20.040
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:23.280
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:27.360
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:30.420
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:33.390
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:37.440
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:42.120
For a timely text on the
pathogens that cause pandemics,

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:44.580
order the e-book,
audiobook, or hard copy

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:47.400
of my last book,
“How to Survive a Pandemic”.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:51.120
For recipes, check out
my second-to-last book,

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.700
my “How Not to Diet Cookbook”.

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:56.820
It’s beautifully designed
with more than 100 recipes

00:12:56.820 --> 00:12:59.160
for delicious and
nutritious meals.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:01.680
And all the proceeds
I receive from the sales

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:04.140
of all my books goes to charity.

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:08.040
NutritionFacts.org is a nonprofit,
science-based public service,

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:10.440
where you can sign up
for free daily updates

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:14.700
on the latest in nutrition research
via bite-sized videos and articles.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.460
Everything on the
website is free.

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:19.860
There’s no ads, no
corporate sponsorship.

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:22.980
It’s strictly non-commercial.
I’m not selling anything.

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:26.400
I just put it up as a public service,
as a labor of love,

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.160
as a tribute to my grandmother,

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:32.820
whose own life was saved
with evidence-based nutrition.

